1: # Actual joint additive pages and their signed returns
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.28 after the parent's complete independent audit. The maps below concern the actual
4: families constructed in P3F.36, P3F.38, P3F.46 and P3F.47, with their specified
5: whole-zero nodal completion and their retained annular line maps. No new
6: comparison between different cusp fans is asserted here.
7: 
8: ## K28.1. The marked norm data and the actual parameter
9: 
10: Place the two capped source fibres at infinity and zero. Write their root
11: orders as \(m_\infty,m_0\), their signed norms as \(n_\infty,n_0\), and put
12: \[
13:  D=m_0n_\infty+m_\infty n_0.
14:  \tag{K28.1}
15: \]
16: The complete integral topology proofs F38, F46 and F47 show that their sphere
17: sector is exactly \(D=\varepsilon\), \(\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}\). In particular
18: \(\gcd(m_\infty,m_0)=1\), and each local pair \((m_i,n_i)\) is primitive.
19: Throughout this proof put \(M=m_\infty m_0\).
20: 
21: The actual source line is denoted \(\mathcal M\), to distinguish it from the
22: integer \(M\). Its contracting lift \(B_\lambda\) covers translation by
23: \(-Q=-bG\). At each resolved root there are actual commuting maps
24: \[
25:  A_{i,0}^{m_i}=B_0^{n_i},\qquad [A_{i,0},B_0]=1 .
26: \]
27: The family in the topology proofs uses
28: \[
29:  \lambda=r^M,\qquad
30:  A_{i,r}=r^{M n_i/m_i}A_{i,0},\qquad
31:  B_\lambda=r^M B_0 .
32:  \tag{K28.2}
33: \]
34: This is the parameter used here. The positive return means one positive
35: loop in \(r\).
36: 
37: Let \(\gamma\) be the outward suspension coordinate, so a positive unit
38: of \(\gamma\) is the inverse contracting map \(B_\lambda^{-1}\). Write
39: \(\alpha=\arg(t)/(2\pi)\) on the ordinary annulus. The actual cap norm
40: coordinates, of periods \(m_i\), are
41: \[
42:  u_0=m_0\gamma-n_0\alpha,\qquad
43:  u_\infty=m_\infty\gamma+n_\infty\alpha.
44:  \tag{K28.3}
45: \]
46: Indeed, on the root \(q_i=z^{m_i}\), the defining cut is
47: \((z,u_i)\mapsto(\zeta_{m_i}z,u_i-n_i)\). Thus a positive source
48: meridian maps to \(-n_i h_i\), and \(\gamma\) maps to \(m_i h_i\),
49: where \(h_i\) is the positive primitive cap circle. This is the actual
50: marked meridian \(m_i\alpha_i+n_i\gamma\) of F38, F46 and F47.
51: The source translations and the integral line characters remain in the
52: other coordinates of these maps.
53: 
54: The norm-shell coordinate can be used with the prescribed boundary metric.
55: To justify this without supposing that a fibrewise lift has a constant
56: pointwise norm, use the actual strictly contracting primitive generator
57: \(H_i\), with \(H_i^{m_i}=B_\lambda\) and \(H_i^{n_i}=A_{i,r}\).
58: An equivariant radial coordinate is chosen on one norm shell and transported
59: by \(H_i\). On the overlap with the flat boundary norm, the increasing lift
60: of the circle-coordinate comparison has \(F(s+1)=F(s)+1\). The radial map
61: \(v\mapsto c^{F(s)-s}v\), in the notation of P3F.31, commutes with the
62: actual contraction and with the physical delta circle. It fixes the source
63: and base coordinates and carries the actual circle line. This gives the
64: same marked norm coordinates (K28.3) through the entire cap.
65: 
66: ## K28.2. An exact map of the orbit base to the weighted three-sphere
67: 
68: On the ordinary annulus set
69: \[
70:  \phi_1=\varepsilon(m_0\gamma-n_0\alpha),\qquad
71:  \phi_2=\varepsilon(m_\infty\gamma+n_\infty\alpha).
72:  \tag{K28.4}
73: \]
74: The phase matrix, with columns \((\gamma,\alpha)\), has determinant
75: \(D=\varepsilon\). Its entries are integers, so it is an actual
76: automorphism of the marked two-torus. Moreover
77: \[
78:  m_0\phi_2-m_\infty\phi_1=\alpha.
79:  \tag{K28.5}
80: \]
81: Choose the unique positive radii satisfying
82: \[
83:  r_1^2+r_2^2=1,\qquad
84:  \frac{r_2^{m_0}}{r_1^{m_\infty}}=|t|.
85: \]
86: The ratio is strictly increasing as \(r_2\) increases from zero to one.
87: The map \(z_j=r_j e^{2\pi i\phi_j}\) therefore identifies the actual
88: ordinary orbit-base annulus with the corresponding annulus in
89: \(S^3\subset\mathbb C^2\), with
90: \[
91:                          t=\frac{z_2^{m_0}}{z_1^{m_\infty}}.
92:  \tag{K28.6}
93: \]
94: 
95: Here are its extensions across the two actual cap solid tori. At infinity,
96: \(q_\infty=1/t=z^{m_\infty}\), let \(R>0\) be defined by
97: \[
98:  R^2+|z|^2R^{2m_0/m_\infty}=1.
99: \]
100: The extending map is
101: \[
102:  (z,u_\infty)\longmapsto
103:  \left(
104:  zR^{m_0/m_\infty}e^{2\pi i\varepsilon m_0u_\infty/m_\infty},
105:  Re^{2\pi i\varepsilon u_\infty}
106:  \right).
107:  \tag{K28.7}
108: \]
109: Under the actual \(A_\infty\) cut the first phase changes by
110: \[
111:  \frac{1-\varepsilon m_0n_\infty}{m_\infty}
112:        =\varepsilon n_0,
113: \]
114: and the second by \(-\varepsilon n_\infty\). Both are integers.
115: Thus (K28.7) descends through the actual marked quotient. Its inverse first
116: recovers \(R=|z_2|\), then a lift of \(\arg(z_2)/(2\pi)\), and finally
117: \(z\). The possible lifts are identified by precisely the powers of
118: the displayed \(A_\infty\) cut. The derivative of the radial equation
119: with respect to \(R\) is positive, so the map and inverse are smooth.
120: 
121: At zero, \(q_0=t=z^{m_0}\), use
122: \[
123:  R^2+|z|^2R^{2m_\infty/m_0}=1
124: \]
125: and the map
126: \[
127:  (z,u_0)\longmapsto
128:  \left(
129:  Re^{2\pi i\varepsilon u_0},
130:  zR^{m_\infty/m_0}e^{2\pi i\varepsilon m_\infty u_0/m_0}
131:  \right).
132:  \tag{K28.8}
133: \]
134: The two phase changes under \(A_0\) are
135: \(-\varepsilon n_0\) and
136: \((1-\varepsilon m_\infty n_0)/m_0=\varepsilon n_\infty\).
137: The same inverse argument applies. Substitution of (K28.3) shows that
138: (K28.7) and (K28.8) agree exactly with (K28.4) on both ordinary overlaps.
139: These formulas, rather than the abstract Seifert invariants alone,
140: give the required global marked orbit-base identification.
141: 
142: The positive weighted Hopf angle, of weights
143: \((m_0,m_\infty)\), is
144: \[
145:                               \beta=\varepsilon\gamma.
146:  \tag{K28.9}
147: \]
148: 
149: ## K28.3. The physical parameter cochain and the complex page
150: 
151: The annular arrow is ordinary-to-cap, as fixed in the construction proofs.
152: If \(c=\log\lambda/(2\pi i)\) and \(s_i=\log q_i/(2\pi i)\), its scalar
153: variation is \(n_i(c-c_0)s_i\delta/m_i\). For a positive \(r\)-loop put
154: \[
155:  k_\infty=m_0n_\infty,\qquad k_0=m_\infty n_0.
156: \]
157: The actual inverse closing multiplier is
158: \[
159:                       f(t)=t^{k_0}(t-t_c)^{-(k_\infty+k_0)}.
160:  \tag{K28.10}
161: \]
162: Its orders at zero and infinity are \(k_0,k_\infty\), respectively.
163: In the sphere sector \(k_\infty+k_0=\varepsilon\).
164: At the selected cusp \(q=t-t_c\), the positive deck is therefore the
165: inverse of (K28.10), and its logarithmic part is
166: \(+\varepsilon\log q\,\delta/(2\pi i)\).
167: The remaining factor is a specified nonvanishing unit. Its logarithm is
168: added to both compared origins, so it cancels in their difference.
169: 
170: For clarity the full cusp origin computation is retained. Put
171: \[
172:  s=\frac{\log q}{2\pi i},\qquad
173:  v=\frac{\log r}{2\pi i},\qquad
174:  \gamma=(g,-C-s-Mv).
175: \]
176: The physical line coordinate is \(V=e^{2\pi i\zeta_2}\), the source
177: Abel coordinate is \(\zeta_1\), and
178: \[
179:  B_\lambda(a,V)=(a-g,e^{2\pi iC}q r^M V).
180: \]
181: Thus the displayed \(\gamma\) is exactly \(B_\lambda^{-1}\).
182: Use the two origins
183: \[
184:  \begin{split}
185:  o_\varepsilon&=\varepsilon
186:        \left(-\frac{gs}{M},\frac{Cs}{M}+\frac{s^2}{2M}+sv\right),\\
187:  \sigma_\varepsilon&=\varepsilon
188:        \left(gv,-Cv-\frac{M(v^2-v)}2\right).
189:  \end{split}
190:  \tag{K28.11}
191: \]
192: They have the actual parameter deck just found, and their difference
193: \(F_\varepsilon=\sigma_\varepsilon-o_\varepsilon\) satisfies
194: \[
195:  F_\varepsilon(v+\theta)-F_\varepsilon(v)
196:    =\varepsilon\theta\gamma
197:       -\frac{\varepsilon M\theta(\theta-1)}2\,\delta .
198:  \tag{K28.12}
199: \]
200: The quadratic delta gauge is zero at both endpoints. On the auxiliary
201: cusp root \(q=Q^{2M}\), the whole angular difference changes by
202: \(-2\varepsilon M\theta\delta\) under \(s\mapsto s+2M\); this is the
203: retained degree-\(2M\) boundary gauge. That auxiliary source root does
204: not change the parameter. One positive \(r\)-loop moves by
205: \(\varepsilon\gamma=\beta\), with the same positive sign for either
206: sphere determinant.
207: 
208: Choose a regular value \(\tau\), distinct from the capped sites and
209: the selected cusp. The actual complex page is
210: \[
211:  \mathcal C^\circ:\quad \eta^{m_0}-\tau\xi^{m_\infty}=1.
212:  \tag{K28.13}
213: \]
214: Its weighted radial embedding into the above three-sphere is
215: \[
216:  (\xi,\eta)\longmapsto
217:        (\rho^{m_0}\xi,\rho^{m_\infty}\eta),
218:  \qquad
219:  \rho^{2m_0}|\xi|^2+\rho^{2m_\infty}|\eta|^2=1 .
220:  \tag{K28.14}
221: \]
222: It is the positive-argument page of the weighted homogeneous polynomial
223: \(z_2^{m_0}-\tau z_1^{m_\infty}\), and its map to the source base is
224: \[
225:                       t=\tau+\xi^{-m_\infty}.
226:  \tag{K28.15}
227: \]
228: The affine curve is smooth: its two partial derivatives cannot both
229: vanish on (K28.13). Its map has degree \(M\). Over infinity there are
230: \(m_0\) points, each of ramification order \(m_\infty\); over zero there
231: are \(m_\infty\) points, each of order \(m_0\); over the regular binding
232: \(\tau\) there is one point of order \(M\). The last assertion follows
233: also from \(\gcd(m_\infty,m_0)=1\): the expansion
234: \(\xi\sim c w^{-m_0}\), \(\eta\sim d w^{-m_\infty}\)
235: has one place at infinity. Riemann--Hurwitz gives
236: \[
237:                   g(\overline{\mathcal C})
238:                     =\frac{(m_\infty-1)(m_0-1)}2.
239:  \tag{K28.16}
240: \]
241: The selected source \(I_1\) is unramified and has \(M\) separate
242: nodal inverse images.
243: 
244: The page is also the exact graph in each actual root cap. At infinity
245: its phase-\(\phi\) graph is
246: \[
247:  \varepsilon m_0 u_\infty+
248:      \frac{\arg(1-\tau z^{m_\infty})}{2\pi}
249:            =\frac{\phi}{2\pi}\pmod{\mathbb Z},
250:  \tag{K28.17}
251: \]
252: and at zero it is
253: \[
254:  \varepsilon m_\infty u_0+
255:      \frac{\arg(z^{m_0}-\tau)}{2\pi}
256:            =\frac{\phi}{2\pi}\pmod{\mathbb Z}.
257:  \tag{K28.18}
258: \]
259: There are \(M\) sheets before quotienting. The actual \(A_\infty\)
260: changes the sheet index by \(-\varepsilon m_0n_\infty\);
261: there are exactly \(m_0\) orbits of length \(m_\infty\).
262: The \(A_0\) sheet step is \(-\varepsilon m_\infty n_0\),
263: giving \(m_\infty\) orbits of length \(m_0\). For phase zero the
264: holomorphic coordinates are, respectively,
265: \[
266:  \begin{array}{ll}
267:  \displaystyle \xi^{m_\infty}
268:        =\frac{z^{m_\infty}}{1-\tau z^{m_\infty}},
269:  &\displaystyle \eta^{m_0}
270:        =\frac1{1-\tau z^{m_\infty}},\\[6pt]
271:  \displaystyle \xi^{m_\infty}
272:        =\frac1{z^{m_0}-\tau},
273:  &\displaystyle \eta^{m_0}
274:        =\frac{z^{m_0}}{z^{m_0}-\tau}.
275:  \end{array}
276:  \tag{K28.19}
277: \]
278: Thus these local complex maps do not depend on the signs of the norms.
279: 
280: ## K28.4. The exact line on the page, including the quadratic starred cap
281: 
282: On each orbit of the sheet permutation choose one representative graph
283: \(u_i=u_{i,j}(z)\). The map from the actual resolved root source is
284: \[
285:                         p\longmapsto [p,u_{i,j}(q_i(p))].
286:  \tag{K28.20}
287: \]
288: It extends across every resolved component, since the phase in
289: (K28.17) or (K28.18) is smooth there. A relation preserving this
290: representative norm level has \(n_i k+m_i h=0\). Primitivity gives
291: \(k=m_i j\), \(h=-n_i j\), and the actual identity
292: \(A_i^{m_i j}B_\lambda^{-n_i j}=1\) proves uniqueness. Thus no residual
293: stabilizer or further root quotient remains in (K28.20).
294: For a nonzero vector of the actual root line
295: \[
296:                          N_i=\mathcal O(aG_i'-O_i),
297: \]
298: normalize its norm to \(u_{i,j}(z)\). This is an explicit
299: delta-equivariant map to the principal circle over (K28.20).
300: Consequently its associated complex line is \(N_i\), including its
301: specified lift and all finite characters.
302: 
303: For F46 and F47 this uses the precise line maps of those propositions:
304: the reduced-root contractions with degree-zero exceptional restriction;
305: the good \(IV^*\) and \(III^*\) toric Cartier corrections; the good
306: III exceptional difference; and the cancelling good-II exceptional
307: coefficients. Each correction was proved invariant under the actual
308: translation \(Q\), so the comparison carries \(B_0\) and \(A_{i,0}\).
309: The sheet map does not replace these corrections by numerical degrees.
310: 
311: Here is the additional line map required for F36 and F38. On the
312: quadratic \(I_1^*\) root let \(S'\) be the minimal \(I_2\) model,
313: let \(\beta:\widehat S\to S'\) blow up the four inversion fixed
314: points, and let \(\pi:\widehat S\to S\) be the smooth resolved
315: quotient. The actual source line is
316: \(\mathcal M=\mathcal O(aG-O-L_0)\), with \(a=2\) or \(6\).
317: If \(E_0,E_1\) are the two exceptional curves at the section leaves,
318: the literal Cartier equalities are
319: \[
320:  \begin{split}
321:  \pi^*(aG-O-L_0)&=\widehat P-\widehat O-2E_0,\\
322:  \beta^*(P'-O')&=\widehat P-\widehat O+E_1-E_0,\\
323:  \beta^*N_*&=\pi^*\mathcal M+E_0+E_1 .
324:  \end{split}
325:  \tag{K28.21}
326: \]
327: The actual \(Q=bG\), \(b=2\) or \(6\), exchanges \(E_0,E_1\)
328: and exchanges the other two exceptional curves. Hence (K28.21)
329: is equivariant for the specified suspension and its affine norm lift.
330: The Tate character of \(N_*\) is
331: \[
332:              Z_P=v(z)^a/u(z^2)^{a/2},\qquad Z_P(0)=-1 .
333:  \tag{K28.22}
334: \]
335: Its transitions are units on both \(I_2\) components, and its degree is
336: zero on each. The full F36 norm
337: \(A_*^2=B_\lambda^{n_*}\) includes the retained line eigencharacter.
338: Applying (K28.20) with \(m_*=2\) therefore gives the actual line
339: \(N_*=\mathcal O(aG'-O')\), not the uncorrected pullback of
340: \(\mathcal M\). This proves the needed line identification even at
341: the two section points and all four fixed-point blowups.
342: 
343: On the ordinary part of the page the projection is the unramified
344: source pullback. At the selected nodes use the actual source-line/orbit
345: charts of P3F.9. The page comparison concerns that source line; the
346: Hopf charge around the fixed sphere is retained in the compression
347: and is not extended through the fixed sphere as a trivial line.
348: The regular-binding band construction of P3K.6 and P3F.31 applies
349: in the coordinate \(\beta\) of (K28.9); it keeps the base coordinate
350: and carries the line by the radial maps in K28.1.
351: 
352: We now trivialize the remaining finite-base source torsor relative
353: to this actual binding collar. The integral monodromy coinvariants
354: vanish for a direct reason in each two-cap case. For F46 and F47 let
355: \(v\) be the retained nodal vanishing cycle and let \(A\) be the
356: order-four source monodromy at III or \(III^*\). The trace calculation
357: in those topology proofs gives
358: \[
359:                           |\langle v,Av\rangle|=1.
360:  \tag{K28.23}
361: \]
362: For F38 the starred monodromy is \(-T_u\), and its product with
363: the retained nodal transvection \(T_v\) and the IV monodromy gives
364: \[
365:        -1=-2+\langle u,v\rangle^2,\qquad
366:        |\langle v,(-T_u)v\rangle|=1 .
367:  \tag{K28.24}
368: \]
369: The nodal meridian has trivial sheet permutation in the actual page
370: cover. Its conjugate by the starred or order-four meridian also
371: has trivial sheet permutation, even though that conjugating path
372: itself need not close on the page. Thus both nodal loops lift as
373: closed page loops. Their monodromies are \(T_v,T_{Av}\), with
374: images \(\mathbb Z v,\mathbb Z Av\) for \(T-1\). Equations
375: (K28.23)--(K28.24) make these images the entire integral elliptic
376: lattice. This proves zero page coinvariants without assuming that
377: an arbitrary branched cover preserves the original Mordell--Weil
378: torsion group.
379: 
380: Let \(C\) be the page base with its marked binding boundary,
381: \(j:C_{\rm reg}\to C\), and \(\Lambda\) its elliptic lattice.
382: The quotient \(j_*\Lambda/j_!\Lambda\) is supported at finitely
383: many singular points and has no first cohomology. Compact-support
384: Poincare duality and the preceding coinvariants give
385: \[
386:                   H^2(C,\partial C;j_*\Lambda)=0 .
387:  \tag{K28.25}
388: \]
389: The smooth identity-component Lie sheaf is fine. Its lattice
390: exponential sequence, together with the finite component sheaf
391: at the singular fibres, consequently gives a translation
392: trivialization of the actual torsor relative to the specified
393: binding collar. Every translation used is a section of the sheaf
394: of translations extending on the actual reduced resolved fibres.
395: The \(I_2\) root is included: its identity group is multiplicative,
396: its lattice at the node is the invariant \(j_*\Lambda\), and its
397: component quotient is the finite skyscraper just retained.
398: 
399: The line comparison through this smooth translation is the
400: rigidified fibrewise Picard comparison of P3K.26, rather than
